(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-4-appendicular-skeleton-quiz"]={"title":"The Appendicular Skeleton — Anatomy Exam 4","category":"Anatomy","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"What two bones make up the pectoral girdle?","choices":["The clavicle and scapula","The scapula and hip bone","The humerus and scapula","The clavicle and sternum"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pectoral girdle consists of the clavicle and scapula. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which end of the clavicle is the sternal end?","choices":["The superior end","The lateral end","The inferior end","The medial end"],"answer":3,"exp":"The sternal end of the clavicle is its medial end, articulating with the sternum. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which landmark is found on the inferior surface of the clavicle near its acromial (lateral) end?","choices":["Coracoid process","Sternal facet","Costal tuberosity","Conoid tubercle"],"answer":3,"exp":"The conoid tubercle is located on the inferior surface of the clavicle near the acromial (lateral) end. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"On the posterior surface of the scapula, what feature separates the supraspinous fossa from the infraspinous fossa?","choices":["The glenoid cavity","The coracoid process","The scapular spine","The medial border"],"answer":2,"exp":"The scapular spine is the ridge that separates the supraspinous fossa (above it) from the infraspinous fossa (below it) on the posterior surface. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is the glenoid cavity, and where is it located?","choices":["A ridge running along the entire posterior surface of the scapula","A shallow socket on the lateral scapula that articulates with the head of the humerus","A small notch located along the superior border of the scapula","A deep fossa on the anterior scapula that anchors the subscapularis muscle"],"answer":1,"exp":"The glenoid cavity is the shallow, lateral socket of the scapula that articulates with the head of the humerus, forming the glenohumeral joint. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which scapular landmark is located on the superior border and serves as an attachment point, forming a passage with the coracoid process for the suprascapular nerve?","choices":["Suprascapular notch","Lateral angle","Acromion","Infraglenoid tubercle"],"answer":0,"exp":"The suprascapular notch is found on the superior border of the scapula. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which bones make up the skeleton of the upper limb, from proximal to distal?","choices":["Clavicle, scapula, humerus, carpal bones, phalanges","Humerus, ulna and radius, carpal bones, metacarpals, phalanges","Femur, tibia and fibula, tarsal bones, metatarsals, phalanges","Humerus, radius and ulna, tarsal bones, metacarpals, phalanges"],"answer":1,"exp":"The upper limb consists of the humerus, ulna and radius, carpal bones, metacarpals, and phalanges. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which structure on the humerus separates the anatomical neck from the shaft, marking the former growth-plate boundary and a common fracture site?","choices":["The deltoid tuberosity","The intertubercular sulcus","The surgical neck","The radial groove"],"answer":2,"exp":"The surgical neck of the humerus lies just distal to the anatomical neck and tubercles, marking the region distal to the (now-fused) growth plate — a common fracture site. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which two distal humeral landmarks articulate with the radius and ulna respectively?","choices":["Capitulum (radius) and trochlea (ulna)","Medial epicondyle (radius) and lateral epicondyle (ulna)","Trochlea (radius) and capitulum (ulna)","Olecranon fossa (radius) and coronoid fossa (ulna)"],"answer":0,"exp":"The capitulum articulates with the head of the radius, while the trochlea articulates with the trochlear notch of the ulna. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which part of the ulna pivots on the trochlea of the humerus?","choices":["The ulnar tuberosity","The trochlear notch","The radial notch","The ulnar styloid process"],"answer":1,"exp":"The trochlear notch of the ulna pivots on the trochlea of the humerus, forming the main hinge of the elbow. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What is the interosseous membrane of the forearm?","choices":["A sheet of connective tissue linking the radius to the carpal bones","A fibrous sheet connecting the ulna and radius along their shafts","A membrane that covers and cushions all eight carpal bones","A short ligament connecting the humerus directly to the ulna"],"answer":1,"exp":"The interosseous membrane is a fibrous sheet between the ulna and radius, connecting their shafts along the interosseous borders. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What movement occurs when the radius crosses over the ulna?","choices":["Flexion","Extension","Supination","Pronation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Pronation is the movement in which the radius crosses over the ulna. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which distal radial landmark receives the head of the ulna?","choices":["Ulnar notch of the radius","Radial tuberosity area","Radial styloid process","Neck of the radius bone"],"answer":0,"exp":"The ulnar notch of the radius, at its distal end, receives the head of the ulna at the distal radio-ulnar joint. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"How many carpal bones make up the wrist, and how are they arranged?","choices":["6 bones, arranged in a single continuous row","8 bones, arranged in a single row of eight","8 bones, in two rows of four (proximal and distal)","10 bones, arranged in two rows of five each"],"answer":2,"exp":"There are 8 carpal bones, arranged in two rows of four: a proximal row and a distal row. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which four carpal bones make up the distal row?","choices":["Lunate, triquetrum, trapezoid, hamate","Trapezium, trapezoid, capitate, hamate","Scaphoid, lunate, triquetrum, pisiform","Scaphoid, trapezium, capitate, pisiform"],"answer":1,"exp":"The distal carpal row consists of the trapezium, trapezoid, capitate, and hamate. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which metacarpal number is associated with the pollex (thumb)?","choices":["Metacarpal I","Metacarpal II","Metacarpal V","Metacarpal III"],"answer":0,"exp":"Metacarpal I is associated with the pollex (thumb). — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What is a coxal bone made of?","choices":["The femur and the acetabular rim","The ilium, ischium, and pubis","The pubis bone and the coccyx","The ilium bone and the sacrum only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Each coxal (hip) bone is made of three fused bones: the ilium, ischium, and pubis. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What is the acetabulum?","choices":["A stout ligament that binds the two pubic bones together anteriorly at the pubic symphysis","The wide opening bordered by the ischium and pubis that transmits the obturator nerve and vessels","The deep socket on the coxal bone where the ilium, ischium, and pubis converge, articulating with the femoral head","The anterior fibrocartilaginous joint connecting the two coxal bones at the midline"],"answer":2,"exp":"The acetabulum is the deep socket formed where the ilium, ischium, and pubis converge, and it articulates with the head of the femur at the hip joint. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Where do the two pubic bones meet anteriorly?","choices":["The pubic symphysis","The acetabulum","The obturator foramen","The greater sciatic notch"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pubic symphysis is the joint where the two pubic bones meet anteriorly, at the pubic crest. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which structure is a large notch on the posterior ischium, providing passage for the sciatic nerve?","choices":["Greater sciatic notch","Lesser sciatic notch","Ischial tuberosity","Obturator foramen"],"answer":0,"exp":"The greater sciatic notch is the large posterior notch through which the sciatic nerve passes. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What is the iliac fossa?","choices":["A bony ridge running along the pelvic brim margin","The rough outer (lateral) surface of the ilium","The smooth, concave inner (medial) surface of the ilium","The deep socket that receives the femoral head"],"answer":2,"exp":"The iliac fossa is the smooth, concave medial (internal) surface of the ilium, providing origin for the iliacus muscle. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What structures make up the bony pelvis?","choices":["Two coxal bones, one sacrum, and one coccyx","Only the sacrum and the coccyx bones","Two coxal bones together with the femur","Two coxal bones and the lumbar vertebrae"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pelvis consists of two coxal bones, one sacrum, and one coccyx. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Which pelvic feature is described as an adaptation for childbearing in the female pelvis?","choices":["Increased sacral and coccygeal curvature compared with the male pelvis","A wider, more circular pelvic inlet with an enlarged pelvic outlet","A narrower subpubic angle than seen in the male pelvis","A narrower pelvic inlet paired with a reduced pelvic outlet"],"answer":1,"exp":"A wider, more circular pelvic inlet and an enlarged pelvic outlet are among the female pelvic adaptations that support childbearing. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Which femoral landmark is a small pit on the head, marking the attachment of the ligament of the head of the femur?","choices":["Greater trochanter","Lesser trochanter","Fovea","Intertrochanteric line"],"answer":2,"exp":"The fovea is the small pit on the femoral head marking the attachment of the ligament of the head of the femur. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which two femoral condyles are found at the distal end and articulate with the tibia?","choices":["Head and neck","Greater and lesser trochanters","Medial and lateral condyles","Medial and lateral epicondyles"],"answer":2,"exp":"The medial and lateral condyles are the distal femoral structures that articulate with the tibia at the knee joint. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What type of bone is the patella, and what is its function?","choices":["An irregular bone located deep within the bony pelvis","A large sesamoid bone that protects the knee joint","A flat bone that helps protect the abdominal organs","A long bone that bears the full weight of the body"],"answer":1,"exp":"The patella is a large sesamoid bone that develops within the quadriceps tendon and protects the knee joint. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Is the tibia medial or lateral to the fibula?","choices":["Lateral","Posterior","Anterior","Medial"],"answer":3,"exp":"The tibia is medial to the fibula in the leg. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What forms the lateral malleolus of the ankle?","choices":["The distal tibia bone","The talus bone itself","The calcaneus bone itself","The distal fibula"],"answer":3,"exp":"The distal end of the fibula forms the lateral malleolus, the bony prominence on the outer ankle. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which metatarsal number is associated with the hallux (great toe)?","choices":["Metatarsal III","Metatarsal I","Metatarsal II","Metatarsal V"],"answer":1,"exp":"Metatarsal I is associated with the hallux (great toe), paralleling how metacarpal I is associated with the thumb in the hand. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Per the material, what types of information can the skeleton reveal about an individual?","choices":["Racial differences, medical history, body size, muscle mass, age, and sex","Only blood type and inherited genetic disease markers","Only the individual's precise age at the time of death","Only whether the individual sustained a prior fracture"],"answer":0,"exp":"The skeleton can reveal racial differences, medical history, body size, muscle mass, age, and sex. — Anatomy of the Appendicular Skeleton - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 64"}]};
